     run: kubectl get pods
			NAME                          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
			app1-hello-85b659c978-g7dmm   1/1     Running   0          68m
			app1-hello-85b659c978-rf6ws   1/1     Running   0          5s
     
	 -->After deletion of pod app1-hello-85b659c978-99ll4, as we mentioned in app.yaml 2 replicas,
	    it automatically created a new pod- app1-hello-85b659c978-g7dmm. and you can see 
		on terminal when pod app1-hello-85b659c978-99ll4 has been deleted all traffic goes to one active pod only pod- app1-hello-85b659c978-g7dmm
		it does not stopped giving responses and after creation of pod app1-hello-85b659c978-g7dmm traffic goes now on this one as well.
		
		
		
9--> Cleaning Up and Deleting all resources on AWS.
     --> run: kubectl delete -f app.yaml
	     It will delete deployment and service both.
		 
			deployment.apps "app1-hello" deleted from default namespace
			service "app1-hello" deleted from default namespace
			
	 --> run: kubectl delete all --all --all-namespace
	     It will delete everything all namespaces and all the deployment, service, jobs unders these namespaces.
		 
	 --> run: eksctl delete cluster --name hello-cluster --region ap-south-1
	      It will delete cluster hello-cluster
		  
		  